Theorem PL-4a: Step 8a (unwind_cleanup) precedes Step 9 (merge_blocks) — unwind cleanup alters the CFG (invoke / resume edges)

  Preconditions:
    - The AIMS pipeline is a 12-step strictly-ordered sequence per
      Annex E §AIMS §6. PL-4a fixes ONE intra-
      function step boundary: Step 8a (unwind_cleanup) SHALL precede Step 9
      (merge_blocks).
        Step 8: detect/rewrite tail calls — CFG optimization
        Step 8a: unwind_cleanup() — Invoke-unwind RC cleanup
        Step 9: merge_blocks() — CFG cleanup (downgrades trivial Invokes)
    - PL-4a statement (per Annex E §AIMS PL-4a + Annex E §AIMS §6
      "unwind_cleanup() (step 8a) — Invoke-unwind RC cleanup (must precede
      merge)"): unwind_cleanup SHALL precede merge_blocks; unwind cleanup
      adds RC-cleanup work to the Invoke unwind (Resume) edges, and
      merge_blocks's downgrade phase would otherwise collapse those Invoke /
      Resume edges away.
    - PL-4a type signature (per Pipeline.proof lines 43-46, the sorry
      obligation this theorem discharges):
        type : Function -> Pipeline
        unwind_cleanup (Step 8a) SHALL precede merge_blocks (Step 9);
        unwind cleanup alters CFG (invoke / resume edges).
    - Shipped Steps 6-9 driver: ori_arc::pipeline::aims_pipeline::
      postprocess::verify_and_merge (compiler/ori_arc/src/
      pipeline/aims_pipeline/postprocess.rs lines 15-51). It runs, in
      straight-line order:
        Step 6 verify (postprocess.rs line 21),
        Step 7 aims_verify (postprocess.rs line 31),
        Step 8 tail-calls detect + rewrite (postprocess.rs lines 36-37),
        Step 8a unwind_cleanup — `crate::aims::emit_rc::unwind_cleanup::
          add_invoke_unwind_cleanup(func, config.interner)` (postprocess.rs
          line 42),
        Step 9 merge_blocks — `crate::block_merge::merge_blocks(func)`
          (postprocess.rs line 47).
      The unwind_cleanup call (line 42) is the straight-line statement
      immediately before the merge_blocks call (line 47).
    - Shipped unwind pass: ori_arc::aims::emit_rc::unwind_cleanup::
      add_invoke_unwind_cleanup (compiler/ori_arc/src/aims/
      emit_rc/unwind_cleanup/mod.rs lines 30-...). It ALTERS the CFG's
      Invoke / Resume edges: for each Invoke whose unwind block is an empty
      Resume (mod.rs lines 57-67), it inserts `Apply @ori_iter_drop(iter)`
      cleanup into that Resume unwind block (mod.rs lines 5-11). The module
      doc (mod.rs lines 8-11) states the pass makes the unwind path
      non-trivial precisely so that "2. The downgrade_trivial_invokes pass
      in merge_blocks() won't collapse the Invoke into an Apply (losing the
      unwind path). 3. The LLVM codegen emits a proper cleanup landing pad."
    - Shipped unwind ordering doc: ori_arc::aims::emit_rc::unwind_cleanup
      module doc (mod.rs lines 13-18) — "Runs after emit_rc_ops() /
      realize_rc_reuse() (RC instructions present) and before merge_blocks()
      (which would downgrade the Invoke). Specifically, it runs in
      verify_and_merge() right before the merge_blocks() call."
    - Shipped merge pass: ori_arc::block_merge::merge_blocks
      (compiler/ori_arc/src/block_merge/mod.rs lines 71-95+).
      Phase 2 downgrade_trivial_invokes (mod.rs line 79) "convert trivial
      Invokes to Apply + Jump when the unwind block is an empty Resume and
      the normal block has a single predecessor" (mod.rs lines 16-18). If
      unwind_cleanup had NOT first filled the Resume block, downgrade would
      treat the Invoke as trivial and remove the invoke / resume edge,
      dropping the cleanup landing pad. PL-4a's ordering is what keeps the
      Invoke non-trivial across the downgrade.
    - This theorem reasons over the LINEAR per-function pipeline DAG; PL-4a
      is the unwind-edge sibling of PL-3 (Step 5 precedes Step 9) and the
      antecedent of PL-4 (Step 10 follows Step 9): the well-formed post-
      unwind CFG is what phase 2's post-merge walk consumes.

  Soundness property:
    Forall function F processed by verify_and_merge.
      Let pos_8a = position(Step 8a unwind_cleanup on F) and
          pos_9 = position(Step 9 merge_blocks on F)
      in the total intra-function execution order.
      Two conjuncts SHALL hold:

      (P1) Unwind-before-merge:
        pos_8a < pos_9. unwind_cleanup completes — inserting the
        ori_iter_drop cleanup into every empty-Resume Invoke unwind block —
        strictly before merge_blocks runs its downgrade phase.

      (P2) Invoke-edge-preservation:
        Because unwind_cleanup ran first, every Invoke whose unwind path
        needs RC cleanup has a NON-empty Resume block at merge time, so
        merge_blocks's Phase 2 downgrade_trivial_invokes does NOT collapse
        it into Apply + Jump. The invoke / resume CFG edge survives the
        merge, preserving the cleanup landing pad.

  Proof obligation:
    Two-part constructive discharge corresponding to (P1) and (P2), by
    structural induction over the per-function Steps-6-9 chain inside
    verify_and_merge + case analysis on merge_blocks's downgrade phase.

    Part (P1) — Unwind-before-merge (structural induction over the
    verify_and_merge step chain):

      Base case — Step 8a (unwind_cleanup):
        verify_and_merge calls add_invoke_unwind_cleanup(func, ...) as a
        straight-line statement (postprocess.rs line 42), inside a block
        scoped by `tracing::info_span!("unwind_cleanup")` (postprocess.rs
        lines 40-43). Post-condition: every empty-Resume Invoke unwind block
        that needs cleanup now carries an ori_iter_drop Apply (unwind/mod.rs
        lines 30-67).

      Inductive step — no step reorders merge ahead of unwind:
        The only statements between Step 8a (line 42) and Step 9 (line 47)
        are the trace_pipeline_checkpoint call (postprocess.rs line 44) and
        the `tracing::info_span!("merge_blocks")` scope entry (postprocess.rs
        line 46). Neither merges blocks. By sequential Rust statement
        evaluation, Step 9 cannot execute before Step 8a's statement
        completes.

      Termination — Step 9 (merge_blocks):
        merge_blocks(func) (postprocess.rs line 47) is the next CFG-mutating
        statement after unwind_cleanup. pos_8a < pos_9 holds on every path.
        (P1) discharged.

    Part (P2) — Invoke-edge-preservation (case analysis on merge_blocks's
    downgrade):

      Case Invoke with cleanup-bearing unwind block (the PL-4a-protected
      case):
        After unwind_cleanup, the Invoke's Resume unwind block contains an
        ori_iter_drop Apply (unwind/mod.rs lines 30-67). When merge_blocks
        Phase 2 downgrade_trivial_invokes (block_merge/mod.rs line 79)
        evaluates this Invoke, the unwind block is NO LONGER an empty Resume
        (its triviality precondition, block_merge/mod.rs lines 16-18, fails),
        so the Invoke is NOT downgraded. The invoke / resume edge survives;
        the landing pad is preserved.

      Case Invoke with genuinely-empty unwind block (no cleanup needed):
        If no iterator / RC-managed variable is live at the Invoke,
        unwind_cleanup inserts nothing (unwind/mod.rs Phase 2 finds no live
        iterators), the Resume stays empty, and merge_blocks correctly
        downgrades the trivial Invoke to Apply + Jump. This is the intended
        simplification — PL-4a does NOT forbid downgrade where no cleanup is
        owed; it only guarantees cleanup-bearing edges survive. Soundness
        preserved in both branches.

      Case merge-before-unwind (counterfactual — rejected):
        If merge_blocks ran before unwind_cleanup, downgrade_trivial_invokes
        would see the unwind block as an empty Resume and collapse the
        Invoke to Apply + Jump — destroying the invoke / resume edge BEFORE
        unwind_cleanup could attach the ori_iter_drop cleanup. The cleanup
        would then have nowhere to land (no Resume unwind block remains),
        leaking iterator handles on the panic path. The verifier rejects any
        schedule with position(Step 9) <= position(Step 8a).

    Coverage gate — pipeline-step ordered-pair enumeration:

      The verifier models the shipped verify_and_merge step chain as an
      ordered step list and discharges the ordered-pair predicate
      position(Step 8a) < position(Step 9) plus the alters-cfg-edges
      attribute on Step 8a and the downgrades-invokes attribute on Step 9.
      It asserts:
        - Step 8a (unwind_cleanup) is present with the alters-invoke-edges
          attribute.
        - Step 9 (merge_blocks) is present with the downgrades-invokes
          attribute.
        - position(Step 8a) < position(Step 9) in the total order.
        - Step 8a is the LAST CFG-edge-altering step before merge (no step
          between 8a and 9 merges or downgrades).

      Coverage axes:
        Unwind-before-merge (P1): the (Step 8a, Step 9) ordered pair, with
          Step 8a verified to be the immediate pre-merge CFG-edge step.
        Invoke-edge-preservation (P2): Step 8a verified alters-invoke-edges,
          Step 9 verified downgrades-invokes (so the protect-then-downgrade
          ordering is what keeps cleanup-bearing edges alive).

    Negative-direction witness — merge-before-unwind rejected:

      Detailed in the merge-before-unwind case above: scheduling Step 9
      before Step 8a downgrades cleanup-needing Invokes to Apply + Jump
      before the cleanup is attached, dropping the unwind landing pad and
      leaking iterator handles on panic. This is precisely the failure the
      unwind module doc warns about (unwind/mod.rs lines 8-11: the pass
      exists "so that ... the downgrade_trivial_invokes pass in
      merge_blocks() won't collapse the Invoke into an Apply (losing the
      unwind path)"). The verifier rejects any schedule with position(Step
      9) <= position(Step 8a).

    Negative-direction witness — unwind cleanup deferred past merge
    rejected:

      Even with Step 8 (tail calls) correctly before Step 9, deferring the
      unwind cleanup to AFTER merge_blocks would mean merge already
      downgraded the trivial Invokes — the cleanup would find no Resume
      block to populate. The shipped driver runs unwind_cleanup
      (postprocess.rs line 42) before merge_blocks (postprocess.rs line 47)
      with no CFG-mutating step between. The verifier rejects any schedule
      that issues unwind cleanup at a position >= pos_9.

    Cycle-soundness witness — PL-4a is the unwind sibling of PL-3, antecedent
    of PL-4:

      PL-4a fixes the Step-8a-before-Step-9 boundary. It shares the
      pre-merge half of the merge boundary with PL-3 (Step 5 phase 1 before
      Step 9). Its successor is PL-4 (Step 10 phase 2 after Step 9): the
      well-formed post-unwind, post-merge CFG is what phase 2's ArcVarId-
      keyed post-merge walk consumes. The dependency chain is acyclic:
        PL-3 --> PL-4a (both fix the pre-merge side of the Step-9 boundary)
        PL-4a --> PL-4 (unwind-edge cleanup before merge keeps the CFG
                      well-formed for phase 2's post-merge walk)
        PL-4a --> PL-5 (no stale summaries — the cleanup is attached to the
                      live CFG before merge mutates it)
      PL-4a produces nothing PL-3 consumes; no cycle.

    Algorithm-citation declaration:
      property: intra-function pipeline-step ordering (unwind-edge
                           cleanup runs before the CFG merge that would
                           otherwise downgrade the Invoke / Resume edges)
      published: standard "materialize-cleanup-edges-before-CFG-
                           simplification" discipline — exception / landing-
                           pad edges must carry their cleanup work before a
                           simplifier prunes "trivial" unwind paths; matches
                           LLVM's requirement that cleanup landing pads exist
                           before SimplifyCFG runs, from the published reference-compiler corpus
                            Repos
      determinism: deterministic — add_invoke_unwind_cleanup
                           (postprocess.rs line 42) followed by merge_blocks
                           (postprocess.rs line 47) is a fixed straight-line
                           ordering inside verify_and_merge; no schedule
                           freedom exists
      reference impl: ori_arc::pipeline::aims_pipeline::postprocess::
                           verify_and_merge (postprocess.rs Step 8a
                           unwind_cleanup line 42, Step 9 merge_blocks line
                           47) + ori_arc::aims::emit_rc::unwind_cleanup::
                           add_invoke_unwind_cleanup (unwind/mod.rs lines
                           30-67, ordering doc lines 13-18, downgrade-guard
                           rationale lines 8-11) + ori_arc::block_merge::
                           merge_blocks Phase 2 downgrade_trivial_invokes
                           (block_merge/mod.rs line 79, triviality precondition
                           lines 16-18)

    Engines dispatched:
      structural_induction (PRIMARY — structural induction over the
        per-function verify_and_merge step chain per section_07::
        verify_pl4a_unwind_before_merge; the verifier models the shipped
        Steps-6-9 chain as an ordered step list and discharges (P1)
        unwind-before-merge via the ordered-pair predicate position(Step 8a)
        < position(Step 9) and (P2) Invoke-edge-preservation via the
        alters-invoke-edges attribute on Step 8a + the downgrades-invokes
        attribute on Step 9; emits Fail on any ordering or attribute
        violation)
      case_analysis (SECONDARY — per-case grid over the cleanup-bearing
        Invoke case + the genuinely-empty-unwind case + the rejected
        merge-before-unwind case; gracious-accept once structural_induction
        has discharged the ordering obligation per the coverage-manifest PL
        row)

  Expected:
    status: valid
    reason: section_07 structural_induction engine discharges PL-4a via the per-function verify_and_merge ordered pair — (P1) Step 8a unwind_cleanup precedes Step 9 merge_blocks (position(Step 8a) < position(Step 9): add_invoke_unwind_cleanup at postprocess.rs line 42 precedes merge_blocks at postprocess.rs line 47 with no CFG-mutating step between) and (P2) the invoke / resume edge is preserved (unwind_cleanup inserts ori_iter_drop into empty-Resume unwind blocks per unwind/mod.rs lines 30-67 so merge_blocks Phase 2 downgrade_trivial_invokes at block_merge/mod.rs line 79 no longer treats the Invoke as trivial — the cleanup landing pad survives per unwind/mod.rs lines 8-18); case_analysis secondary-accepting per the coverage-manifest PL row
